derivations.claim:
Suppose the characteristic is $p>0$ and put $c=1$ for odd $p$ and $c=2$ for $p=2$. For every positive integer $j$, the order of $1-(1+t)^{2j}$ is $c p^{
u_p(j)}$: factor $2j=p^h u$ with $p
mid u$, use $(1+t)^{p^h}=1+t^{p^h}$, and the coefficient of $t^{p^h}$ is the nonzero residue of $u$. If $q_0^2
e1$, finite $\ell$ is the order of $q_0^2$, and it is prime to $p$ because a field has no nonidentity element of order $p$. Thus $[j]_{\delta(t)}$ has order zero for $\ell
mid j$ and order $c p^{
u_p(j/\ell)}$ for $\ell\mid j$. If $q_0^2=1$, the recurrence gives $[j]_\delta=j q_0^{j-1}$ and $\ell=p$; the denominator $1-q(t)^2$ has order $c$, so the order of $[j]_{\delta(t)}$ is $c(p^{
u_p(j)}-1)$. These statements hold also in characteristic two.
